are
> > not published. You might want to think about that aspect too.)
> 
> Even if it violates the spirit of S and doesn't give away the algorithm - if 
you
> have to make money out of your data analyses, you don't write code and share 
it
> happily. Like the poster said, if there's no way of hiding the code or making 
it
> very difficult to read at least, Splus is not an option any more.
> I assume that many commercial users have thought of this before. I'd like to
> hear more about other approaches.

OK, if I HAD to do this I would

- Make sure the options(error) cannot be dump.frames() ....
- Write a C function and pass the data to that via a call to .C().
- Within that function use call_S to invoke the S functions I needed to
  do the analysis.
- The call_S interface needs the S function to be specified via a pointer. 
  If you really wanted to, the S objects could be kept in encrypted form 
  and decrypted by the C code just before call_S. In any case, they need
  never be visible as S objects.

There are ways to make S objects unlistable, at least by simple means.
Suppose you store them somewhere other than .Data in a non-standard
format, and use a private dget() method (written in C) to retrieve the
object from within the calling function. Then the called object could
be found by dumping from within the caller, but it would be easy to
nest this and lay a false trail.  S4 (and hence I suppose S-PLUS 5) has
.on.error() that can be used to trap interrupts which might cause a
core dump or dump frames. It becomes a question of how hard you are
trying; this may be as safe as compiled C code.  I have not thought
through all the security implications, as S experts who might be able
to do this are probably worth a lot more than your code.

As for the spirit of S, remember that S code is copyrightable like any
other writing, so you may only use it under the licence conditions
under which you received it, which do not necessarily allow reading the
source and may well not allow modification nor re-use.  So there are at
least moral ways to protect your work.  [A recent book contains
verbatim uncredited S code from ours: that is not the spirit of S.] So
adding security implies a view of your client's business ethics, and
may be seen as such. It may also be seen as an indication that you have
something to hide (and I have been employed as a consultant to look
into just such a case by a prospective purchaser).

-- 
