>> (P.S. There are a lot of sensible people who don't trust algorithms which are

>> not published. You might want to think about that aspect too.)

>if you have to make money out of your data analyses, you don't write code and
>share it happily...
>I'd like to hear more about other approaches.

This may not be the model you want to follow, but since you asked, there are
many companies based on a "fee for service" model rather than a "charge for
proprietary rights" model. The industry around Linux and GNU are the most
notable software examples. People and companies do make profits in this
industry. To some extent Splus is also an example. It was more so when S was
easily available and may become so again now that R is a viable alternative.

Another important example is encryption software. It now seems to be generally
accepted that published algorithms are safer than secret algorithms, since they
can be publicly scrutinized. The weaknesses of secret algorithms may be known to
only a few people, but they may be the wrong people.

You have to remember that if more than one customer buys your code then you can
usually make a profit by selling it for less than it would cost them to develop,
debug, support, and maintain it themselves. Of course, this assumes that your
customers would not try to resell it themselves, but as pointed out by Brian
Ripley, copyright should be a deterent to that. Also, the fact that maintenance
is a big part of the cost can be a considerable deterent.

To return to the original point, I personally would have a great deal of
difficult accepting a statistical result based on a secret algorithm. You may
want to reconsider whether this is the model you need to follow.

Paul Gilbert

