>I have not thought
>through all the security implications, as S experts who might be able
>to do this are probably worth a lot more than your code.

It is very unlikely that his code is worth protecting. I've been with two
funds now that used S-plus for critical things. (My current fund uses almost
exclusively S-plus/C/Tcl/Tk and we've returned about 300% over the last 30
months trading, so I'd say our code was worth protecting.) We do not believe
that Mr. Krause can "safely" distribute his code in S-plus, or in any other
form. If it's worth what he hopes, he should run the code himself and manage
money, rather than license it to others who take the risk.

Another aspect is why our fund never uses consultants. When we're trading
around the clock we don't want to uncover a bug when the author is not
immediately available. So we maintain a support staff which are always
available. And we make sure that the authors of any proprietary code do not
have any other principal sources of income than our fund. (This applies to
me, as well as my partner, not just the people who work for us.) It makes a
real difference if someone writing code might lose a very high paying job
relative to their skill/experience level. So we pay a lot, and expect a lot.
Oddly enough it turns out that it's by far the cheapest and most reliable
way to get good proprietary software there is.

Later,
Andrew Mullhaupt

