From: Samuel E. Buttrey
To: asinclair
Subject: Re: [S] strings containing  "\"
Date: 13 August 1998 08:31

Hi. You've done this correctly: "\\." produces
a string with *one* backslash and a dot. It
*looks* like two, when you print it out: that's
because Splus "protects" the one backslash with
another. But there are really only two characters
in there. Try nchar(a) (it's 2) or cat (a, "\n").

I hope this helps,
Sam Buttrey
