Message-ID: <3.0.5.32.19980814122915.005dfa20@mail.wsl.ch>

Hi there,

(For a manova, Y = X*B + U, the matrix X is needed as a result parameter,
for further manipulation, like computingt Yhat - X*B without loosing the
lines with NA.

As it is implemented, manova returns residuals, but only for rows of Y with
not one NA in them. )

Analyzing the code of lm, partial answers to my problem seem available. The
culprit is found in the function call of eval, which leaves out data for
all lines which contain an NA in the response variable Y. So, using $x, $y,
and $model will not help me.

"lm" <-
function(formula, data, weights, subset, na.action, method = "qr", model =
F, x = F, y = F, contrasts = NULL, ...)  {
  call <- match.call()
  m <- match.call(expand = F)
  m$method <- m$model <- m$x <- m$y <- m$contrasts <- m$... <- NULL
  m[[1]] <- as.name("model.frame")
  m <- eval(m, sys.parent())
  if(method == "model.frame")  return(m)
  Terms <- attr(m, "terms")
  weights <- model.extract(m, weights)
  Y <- model.extract(m, response)
  X <- model.matrix(Terms, m, contrasts)
  fit <- if(length(weights)) lm.wfit(X, Y, weights, method, ...) else
lm.fit(X, Y, method, ...)
  fit$terms <- Terms
  fit$call <- call
  if(model)  fit$model <- m
  if(x)  fit$x <- X
  if(y)  fit$y <- Y
  attr(fit, "na.message") <- attr(m, "na.message")
  fit
}

Question:
=======

Can  a substitute for "eval" be found which does NOT suffer from this
restriction?

Thanks for helping.

