From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Fri Aug 14 12:27:57 1998
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian Ripley)
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 1998 13:27:57 +0100
Subject: Re: [S] 2. X wanted for manova, Y = X*B + U
Message-ID: <199808141227.NAA00238@toucan.stats.ox.ac.uk>

From: Christian Hoffmann <christian.hoffmann at wsl.ch>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 1998 12:29:15 +0200

> (For a manova, Y = X*B + U, the matrix X is needed as a result parameter,
> for further manipulation, like computingt Yhat - X*B without loosing the
> lines with NA.
> 
> As it is implemented, manova returns residuals, but only for rows of Y with
> not one NA in them. )

`As it is implemented', manova does not accept rows of Y with missing values 
in them. From the help page example

> wafer[1, "pre.mean"] <- NA
> wafer.manova <- manova(cbind(pre.mean, post.mean) ~ maskdim +
          visc.tem + spinsp, wafer)
Error in function(object, ...): missing values not allowed: found
        in cbind(pre.mean, post.mean)

So, YOU have changed something, probably the na.action function to na.omit.

> Analyzing the code of lm, partial answers to my problem seem available. The
> culprit is found in the function call of eval, which leaves out data for
> all lines which contain an NA in the response variable Y. So, using $x, $y,
> and $model will not help me.

This has nothing to do with eval. This generates a call to model.mframe
with argument na.action, and you must have changed that, or the
call to lm would fail.

> Can  a substitute for "eval" be found which does NOT suffer from this
> restriction?

Yes, but you need to tell us the whole truth of what you are doing.
For example,

wafer[1, "pre.mean"] <- NA
wafer.manova <- manova(cbind(pre.mean, post.mean) ~ maskdim +
          visc.tem + spinsp, wafer, na.action=na.omit)
attach(wafer)
cbind(pre.mean, post.mean) - predict(wafer.manova, newdata=wafer)
    pre.mean  post.mean 
 1        NA -0.0966667
 2  0.012361 -0.1522222
 3 -0.162139  0.0411111
 4  0.170861  0.4011111
 5 -0.297611 -0.8044444
 6  0.265889  0.2788889
 7 -0.372639 -0.3822222
 8  0.485889  0.7422222
 9 -0.102611 -0.1244444
10 -0.038593 -0.0037037
11  0.090935 -0.0692593
12  0.097435  0.1840741
13  0.381435  0.5540741
14 -0.263037  0.2385185
15 -0.257537 -0.6681481
16 -0.141065 -0.5692593
17 -0.028537  0.0451852
18  0.158963  0.2885185

MAY be what you are looking for.  You cna get X via model.matrix, but I
would use the tools available to you like predict.  (I don't know what
would conventionally be meant by a manova of a dataset with missing
values, but I do know that residuals are associated with regressions
not (man)aov, so all you need for this is lm.)

